   "response_text": "Everything confirmed. Let me put together the analysis.\n\n## Findings \ud83d\udc36\n\n**(1) `raise TooManyRedirects` line number**\n\nThere are two raise sites in `httpx/_client.py` \u2014 one in the sync client's `_send_handling_redirects`, one in the async client's. Both are the self-same line:\n\n- Line **972** (sync path)\n- Line **1719** (async path)\n\n**(2) Status codes that can flip the method to GET** \u2014 from `_redirect_method` (lines 473\u2013494):\n\n- `303 SEE_OTHER` \u2192 GET (unless HEAD)\n- `302 FOUND` \u2192 GET (unless HEAD)\n- `301 MOVED_PERMANENTLY` \u2192 GET (only when the method was POST)\n\nSo: **301, 302, 303**\n\n**(3) Headers stripped when the method switches to GET** \u2014 in `_redirect_headers` (lines 540\u2013544), guarded by `method != request.method and method == \"GET\"`:\n\n- `Content-Length` and `Transfer-Encoding`\n\nSo: **Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding**\n\n**(4) The one header removed on every redirect regardless of origin/method**\n\n`_redirect_headers` unconditionally does `headers.pop(\"Cookie\", None)` \u2014 the client cookie store is always used to re-derive cookies instead. (`Authorization` is only stripped cross-origin, and `Host` is only updated cross-origin.)\n\nSo: **Cookie**\n\nFiles were only read \u2014 nothing modified, paw-swear. \ud83d\udc3e\n\nANSWER: 972|301, 302, 303|Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding|Cookie",
